PatchKnob
==============

PatchKnob is a multi-platform SDL DAW and modular synthesis environment built around:

- tracker, piano-roll, and timeline sequencing
- sampler instruments with keyzones and per-sample editing
- VST2/VST3 hosting with parameter automation
- a GPU-drawn modular rack with Cardinal/VCV-style bridge modules
- Pure Data and Csound patch nodes
- project save/load for sequences, patch graphs, rack state, instruments, and embedded audio

## Building on Linux

PatchKnob builds from source on any reasonably current Linux distribution.
Two commands:

```sh
./scripts/install-deps.sh     # system packages for your distro
./scripts/build.sh            # fetch vendored sources, configure, build
```

The binary lands at `build-linux/PatchKnob`.

### What the scripts do

`scripts/install-deps.sh` detects your distribution from `/etc/os-release` and
installs the system packages PatchKnob needs. It knows Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora/
RHEL, Arch, openSUSE and Alpine. On anything else it prints the dependency list
in generic terms so you can translate it, rather than failing silently. Pass
`--dry-run` to see the command without running it.

`scripts/bootstrap-vendor.sh` fetches the third-party sources PatchKnob
compiles into the binary, each **pinned to an exact commit** so a moving
upstream cannot break a build that worked yesterday. `build.sh` runs it for
you; run it directly with `--force` to re-fetch. Some of these trees are large
(Cardinal alone is ~1.8 GB), which is why they are fetched rather than
committed — the repository itself stays small enough to clone quickly.

`scripts/build.sh` configures and builds. Useful flags:

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--debug` | debug build instead of release |
| `--clean` | wipe the build directory first |
| `--jobs N` | limit parallelism |
| `--tests` | also build and run the test suites |
| `--dir D` | build into `D` instead of `build-linux` |

It caps the job count against available RAM by default: a parallel build of
this size is memory-bound rather than CPU-bound, and a machine with many cores
and modest RAM will otherwise swap or get compiler processes OOM-killed. Use
`--jobs` to override.

### Requirements

- CMake 3.16+ and a C++17 compiler (GCC 9+ or Clang 10+)
- ALSA (required). JACK and PulseAudio are optional and detected if present.
- SDL2 (or SDL3) with SDL_ttf, X11, OpenGL
- libcurl — used only by the in-editor assistant, which is off unless you
  enable it
- bison and flex — Csound generates its parser at build time

Everything else is vendored and built for you.

### Running the tests

```sh
./scripts/build.sh --tests
```

Or individually, for example the arrange-view suite:

```sh
cmake -S sdlui/views/arrange -B build-arrange
cmake --build build-arrange -j"$(nproc)"
SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy ./build-arrange/arrange_view_test --selftest
```

### Troubleshooting

**The build is killed partway through.** It ran out of memory. Use
`./scripts/build.sh --jobs 2`.

**`Could NOT find SDL2` / `No package 'sdl2' found`.** Run
`./scripts/install-deps.sh`. If your distro splits headers into a separate
package, you need the `-dev` / `-devel` variant.

**No sound, or the app reports no audio device.** PatchKnob needs ALSA. Check
that your user is in the `audio` group and that another application is not
holding the device exclusively.

**A vendored tree is on the wrong revision.** `./scripts/bootstrap-vendor.sh`
re-pins it; `--force` re-clones from scratch.

