## Linux Build

Install SDL3, SDL3_ttf, libpng, librsvg/cairo, fontconfig, and PortAudio
backend dependencies, then run:

```sh
./tools/build_linux.sh
./build-linux/PatchKnob
```

On Ubuntu/Debian (24.04+ for SDL3 packages):

```sh
sudo apt install build-essential cmake pkg-config python3 libsdl3-dev \
  libsdl3-ttf-dev libpng-dev librsvg2-dev libcairo2-dev libfontconfig1-dev \
  libasound2-dev libjack-jackd2-dev libpulse-dev
```

On Arch/Omarchy:

```sh
sudo pacman -S --needed base-devel cmake pkgconf python sdl3 sdl3_ttf libpng \
  librsvg cairo fontconfig alsa-lib jack2 libpulse
```

(`python3` generates the Fundamental rack modules' DSP source; Csound and its
`libsndfile` dependency build from the vendored source automatically, no
extra packages needed for those.)

If your distro's SDL3 package predates SDL3_ttf, or you need to stay on
SDL2, configure with `-DPATCHKNOB_SDL3=OFF` and install `libsdl2-dev`/
`libsdl2-ttf-dev` (or `sdl2`/`sdl2_ttf`) instead.

Fonts: the shell asks `fontconfig` for the system's "monospace" family at
startup, so any distro with a normal font setup works out of the box. Only
if fontconfig itself is unavailable does it fall back to a short list of
hardcoded Debian-style paths (`/usr/share/fonts/truetype/...`), which will
not resolve on Arch/Fedora/NixOS -- so keep `libfontconfig1-dev`/`fontconfig`
installed at build time.

VST2/VST3 hosting, the Fundamental rack modules, and Csound all work the same
way on Linux as on Windows:

- **VST2** loads Linux `.so` plugins (not Windows `.dll`s -- those still only
  load on Windows) via `dlopen`, with the same in-process crash containment
  as Windows (`seh_guard_posix.cpp`: a POSIX `sigaction`/`sigsetjmp` guard
  standing in for Windows' VEH, so a faulting plugin doesn't take the whole
  app down). Scanned from `/usr/lib/vst`, `/usr/local/lib/vst`, `~/.vst`.
- **VST3** loads standard cross-platform `.vst3` bundles via the vendored
  SDK's own Linux hosting module. Scanned from `/usr/lib/vst3`,
  `/usr/local/lib/vst3`, `~/.vst3`.
- **Fundamental** (VCO/VCF/ADSR/LFO/Mixer/...) rack modules run real DSP, not
  stubs: the Windows build's `generate_fundamental_dsp.ps1` code-generation
  step (extracting the DSP-only part of each vendored VCV Rack module source)
  has a `python3` port (`tools/generate_fundamental_dsp.py`) used automatically
  when PowerShell isn't found, so `python3` is a Linux build dependency too.
- **Csound** is built from the vendored source (`vendor/csound`) and
  statically linked -- no system Csound install needed, and none of the
  DLL-staging Windows needs (`CSOUND_ROOT` still works if you'd rather point
  at a real install/prefix instead).

### Running under KMS/DRM (no desktop, no window manager)

PatchKnob's SDL shell is built as an "appliance" target: every file/save/
folder picker is drawn by the app itself (see `sdlui/views/file_dialog/`),
and the toolkit includes its own in-app window manager
(`ui::WindowManager` in `sdlui/gui.h`) for exactly this case, so nothing
requires an OS window manager, taskbar, or native dialog to run.

To boot straight into PatchKnob on bare hardware (no X11/Wayland session):

1. The SDL3 build must have its `kmsdrm` video backend compiled in.
   Distro SDL3 packages built against `libdrm`/`libgbm` (mesa) already
   include it -- check with:
   ```sh
   SDL_VIDEODRIVER=kmsdrm ./build-linux/PatchKnob
   ```
   `SDL_Init: kmsdrm not available` means the installed SDL3 was built
   without it; rebuild SDL3 from source with `-DSDL_KMSDRM=ON` (needs
   `libdrm-dev`/`libgbm-dev` or their Arch equivalents `libdrm`/`mesa`) and
   point `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` at that build.
2. The user running PatchKnob needs permission to become DRM master on the
   card in `/dev/dri` -- either membership in the `video` group
   (`sudo usermod -aG video $USER`, then re-login) or a running `seatd`/
   `logind` session that grants it.
3. Nothing else may already hold DRM master on that card. A desktop
   compositor (Hyprland, GNOME, KDE, a display manager) claims it while
   running, so KMS/DRM only succeeds from a bare VT with no compositor
   active (e.g. `systemctl isolate multi-user.target`, or a distro image
   with no display manager at all) -- not alongside your normal desktop
   session.

Once those hold, `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=kmsdrm ./build-linux/PatchKnob` (or simply
running it with no other video session active, since SDL falls back to
`kmsdrm` automatically when there is no X11/Wayland to find) opens directly
on the framebuffer.

## Windows Build

Build from an MSYS2 MINGW64 shell or a PowerShell with MSYS2 on `PATH`:

```sh
export PATH="/c/msys64/mingw64/bin:/c/msys64/usr/bin:$PATH"
cmake -S . -B build -G "MinGW Makefiles"
cmake --build build --config Release
./build/PatchKnob.exe
```

The Windows build stages the runtime DLL closure next to the executable in `build/`.

## Installer

Create the NSIS installer with:

```sh
cmake --build build --target nsis_installer --config Release
```

The installer is written to:

```text
build/installer/PatchKnob-0.8.7-Setup.exe
```

## Rack Modules

The app loads rack bridge modules from a `modules/` directory next to the
executable, or from the path in `PATCHKNOB_RACK_MODULE_DIR` for compatibility with
older module builds.

